# Pending human-applied patch — issue #731 (PM gate② finding, PR #818)
#
# WHAT: extends guard.ts's Bash-argument accident fence to recognize the three stop-control
#       CLI verbs #731 shipped — `sapwood pause`, `sapwood stop`, `sapwood estop` (activate AND
#       `clear` forms) — as human-merge-only, the SAME boundary CONTROL_SENTINEL_RE already
#       enforces for the raw files (data/PAUSE, data/KILL_SWITCH, data/EMERGENCY_STOP). Adds one
#       new function pair (isSapwoodCliEntrypoint / checkStopControlVerb) wired into
#       judgeFragment, plus a BLOCK/ALLOW test matrix in guard.test.ts. Scope is deliberately
#       minimal (大道至简): ONLY the three stop-control verbs, activate and clear forms — `park`/
#       `run`/`status`/`events` are explicitly OUT of scope (a worker has ordinary legitimate
#       reason to invoke those; widening this rule to every subcommand is a non-goal, not an
#       oversight — the ALLOW matrix below proves it).
#
# WHY: #731 (PR #818, this same round) added first-class `sapwood pause|stop|estop` CLI verbs as
#       thin wrappers over the three control-sentinel files. They resolve the sentinel path
#       INTERNALLY (dirname(dbPath) + a fixed filename) rather than taking it as a literal
#       command-line argument, so `checkControlSentinelArg` — which closes exactly this class of
#       indirection for a hand-rolled `node some-script.js <sentinel-path>` — cannot see them:
#       no sentinel path ever appears as a token on the Bash command line. #779/#809 just closed
#       the raw touch/rm class (a worker forging/deleting a control sentinel directly); #731
#       shipped, in the SAME PR that introduced the verbs, an equally first-class, operator-
#       documented, easy-to-run bypass of that closure — `sapwood estop clear` lifts an already-
#       fired EMERGENCY_STOP with no sentinel path in sight, and a worker with ordinary Bash
#       access can flip any tier by running the well-known `sapwood` command, no bespoke script
#       needed. PM gate② review of PR #818 flagged this as a P1 the same PR that opens the gap
#       must close (or, since guard.ts is human-merge-only, ship as a paste-ready patch): #731's
#       own docs/security.md note describes this as "the existing documented residual class,"
#       but it is not — the earlier residual is an obscure hand-rolled form; this is a first-
#       class, operator-documented, worker-discoverable command. This patch is the accident
#       fence's extension to cover it.
#
# WHY IT IS A PATCH AND NOT A COMMIT: engine/src/guard/** is human-merge-only (CLAUDE.md
#       "Non-negotiables"; canonical list docs/security.md "Human-merge-only paths"). An
#       autonomous worker's write to guard.ts/guard.test.ts is denied at the tool level:
#         BLOCK [write-path] guard/reviewer/merge-driver source is human-merge-only
#       The guard constrains Claude tool calls, never a human's editor.
#
# APPLY ORDER NOTE (docs/patches/679-guard-default-branch-push.patch may land adjacent in the
#       same file, if it exists in your tree): this patch's own hunks were deliberately
#       positioned AWAY from judgeFragment's tail (where #679 rewrites the final `return
#       scanGhOverreach(...)` line into an if/return + its own git-push check) — this patch
#       inserts its call site BEFORE checkControlSentinelArg's call instead of after, and its
#       new function pair immediately after checkControlSentinelArg's own definition, neither of
#       which #679 touches. Verified empirically both ways: `git apply` succeeds applying #679
#       first then this patch, AND applying this patch first then #679 — both orders produce a
#       structurally correct judgeFragment (this patch's checkStopControlVerb call runs before
#       checkControlSentinelArg's; #679's git-push check runs after the gh-overreach check,
#       unaffected either way). Apply in whichever order is convenient; no reconciliation needed
#       for either sequence. If a FUTURE, unrelated change to guard.ts's judgeFragment body has
#       since altered the surrounding context, re-verify with `git apply --check` before trusting
#       either order blindly.
#
# APPLY (from the repo root), then delete this file AND its apply-check test:
#       git apply docs/patches/731-guard-stop-control-verbs.patch
#       git rm docs/patches/731-guard-stop-control-verbs.patch
